    "collection": "ranked",
    "rank": 263,
    "domain": "quantum_information_computation",
    "domain_label": "Quantum information & computation",
    "exact_target": "Determine whether QMA(2)=QMA in the ordinary unrelativized, uniform polynomial-time quantum verification model. For a promise problem (Y,N), QMA(2) allows two polynomial-size quantum witnesses, required to be a product across the two prover registers: each YES input has some such witness accepted with probability at least 2/3, and each NO input is accepted with probability at most 1/3 for every such witness. QMA allows one unrestricted polynomial-size quantum witness with the same bounds. There is no requirement outside the promise. The verifier uses a fixed standard universal quantum computation model, with no oracle or advice. The two complete outcomes are equality and strict containment QMA properly contained in QMA(2).",
    "link": "https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v005a001/v005a001.pdf",
