    "publication_id": "proofatlas.open-problem-ranking.top500.v22",
    "reader_question": "For a smooth planar billiard table whose boundary has positive curvature everywhere, suppose all trajectories sufficiently close to grazing the boundary are organized by a continuous foliation of invariant closed curves, each winding once around phase space. Must the table be an ellipse, including a circle?",
    "why_it_matters": "A proof would recover the table\u2019s shape from this precise local organization of trajectories, without requiring a foliation of all phase space. A counterexample would provide a nonelliptic table with the same near-boundary property.",
