    "rank": 421,
    "domain": "geometry_and_topology",
    "domain_label": "Geometry & topology",
    "exact_target": "For each integer n>=1, are there only finitely many deformation-equivalence classes of compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic complex manifolds of complex dimension 2n? Such a manifold is smooth, simply connected and compact K\u00e4hler, with H^0(X,Omega_X^2) spanned by a holomorphic two-form nondegenerate everywhere. Deformation equivalence is the equivalence generated by occurrence as fibers in smooth proper holomorphic families of such manifolds over connected bases. No projectivity, polarization, lattice, second-Betti-number or fibration restriction is imposed. Resolve by proving finiteness in every fixed dimension or showing infinitely many deformation classes in one fixed dimension.",
    "link": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.03215",
