    "rank": 429,
    "domain": "number_theory_arithmetic_geometry",
    "domain_label": "Number theory & arithmetic geometry",
    "exact_target": "For every dimension n>=1, every real nonsingular n by n matrix A with |det A|=1 and every translation x in R^n, there exists an integer vector m in Z^n such that the product over i=1,...,n of |x_i+(Am)_i| is at most 2^(-n). The quantifier requires an ACTUAL integer witness at the closed bound, not merely an infimum no greater than the bound. Equivalently, scaling a general real nonsingular matrix B and its translation by |det B|^(-1/n) gives the same actual-witness assertion with bound |det B|/2^n; all coefficients and translations are real. No equality characterization of extremal lattices, well-rounded Euclidean covering assertion, positive-characteristic analogue or constructive algorithm is appended. Preserve all dimensions, not merely a selected high-dimensional open remainder.",
