    "domain_label": "Probability, ergodic theory & dynamics",
    "exact_target": "For every non-atomic probability law G on [0,infinity) such that E[min(t_1,t_2,t_3,t_4)^2]<infinity for four independent G-distributed variables, assign independent G-distributed passage times t_e to the unoriented nearest-neighbor edges of Z^2. Is it almost surely the case that there is no doubly infinite self-avoiding nearest-neighbor path (v_i)_{i in Z} for which, for every i<j, the segment from v_i to v_j minimizes the sum of edge passage times over all finite nearest-neighbor paths with those endpoints? The probability is for the product edge law. This is the standard minimum-moment version selected from the legacy canonical finite-moment scope using Damron\u2013Hanson A1' and Ahlberg\u2013Hoffman A1, not a claim of equivalence to Damron\u2013Hanson's separately stated moment-free conjecture. No first moment of a single edge, exponential moment, absolute continuity of a density, curvature assumption, or deterministic asymptotic direction is imposed.",
